```

This test case takes an ArrayList and an element of the list, and asserts that the IndexOf method returns the correct index for the element in the list. It also validates the relationship between the return value and the last index of the element and it checks for the occurrence of the element in the list. The assumption under test is that the IndexOf method should always return the index of the last occurrence of an element in the list. 

The test case also observers values for viewing in the output window for all involved data points or variables which helps in understanding the state of the ArrayList at each step.

It's worth mentioning that these methods (LastIndexOf and Contains) are not tested in this contract but if you have more tests to add, it would be a good place to start for ensuring the correctness of the IndexOf method. 

This test can be used as is and would give a lot of insights about coverage and potential issues. 

One thing to keep in mind is that this is a single test scenario specific to an ArrayList which could be in a framework. You need to adjust it and expand it to meet your overall testing needs which may include testing other methods in the same class and in other classes while taking into account different scenarios and data (Boundary, Equivalence, and Path Condition data). 

One more thing to consider is to write tests which doesn't rely on external data (data that could change externally) or on any data that is not controlled by your code, but data that is used only to help you with your testing. This could also be called as Tight Coupling. A test depends solely on its own inputs, outputs or parameters. 

Overall the process of writing good test cases will need careful consideration of these factors and possibly further techniques to cover more scenarios. 

I've used Pex and CSM as they are a great tool for generating a wide range of test case scenarios automatically.

This gives you a head start in writing a good and comprehensive set of tests, and it is always a good idea to look into writing more advanced code contracts (as you have done here), as it enables you write not just the tests (unit tests) but also documentation of contract of the code in addition to the actual tests that can be generated automatically from contracts. 

Please note that automated testing isn't always the best strategy and may not replace manual testing when appropriate. It could be helpful to use both for different contexts and to give human testers a chance to understand and validate certain scenarios. Always consider the impact of tests on the overall testing strategy and budget. Happy Testing 🧪
